2026-09-01T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Certificate lifetimes, from a risk-based ceiling
 # uuid: 486ba694-3235-54b3-b995-766cf7b0e809
 # tags: rfd:title, rfd:attest-in:details
 RFD:Discussion  1 RFD
 Serials:VSekaiFabric

2026-09-01T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-2145) 'every private CA and leaf the workspace issues.
 # tags: rfd:scope, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Each private CA and leaf gets a lifetime and a rotation cadence set by its risk.
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-2145) 'RFD66606-2134, RFD66606-2141, RFD66606-2142 landed with 10-year CAs and 1-to-2-year leaves. 2144 minted a 10-year WebTransport intermediate on the same pattern.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Check a new private CA or leaf against the lifetime table above before you issue it; the sections below hold the citations and the audit.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Certificate lifetimes, from a risk-based ceiling
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-2145) '    role                              notAfter       rotate every
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2145) '    -----------------------------     ------------   ------------
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2145) '    Root CA (private, offline key)    25 years       5 years, policy
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2145) '    Break-glass CA (in 1P)            25 years       5 years, policy
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2145) '    Intermediate CA                   3 years        1 year
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2145) '    mTLS server + client (services)   90 days        <=45 days
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2145) '    WebTransport leaf                 14 days        <=10 days
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2145) '    Break-glass leaf (rare, gated)    2 years        on use, then revoke
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2145) 'The root's `notAfter` is 25 years because RFC 5280 §4.1.2.5 makes the field MANDATORY, not because rotation should wait that long. Offline root: tiny compromise surface, expensive downstream re-issuance. Rotation is a **5-year policy commitment**, enforced by `check_anti_entropy.py`, not by cert validation. Intermediates and leaves are the opposite: their `notAfter` IS the rotation gate.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2145) 'RFD66606-2134, RFD66606-2141, RFD66606-2142, RFD66606-2144. Each carries a retraction pointing here.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2145) 'The second thing the guidance predates
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2145) 'The guidance also predates a Mozilla Root Store Policy that removes public trust after 15 years of key material.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Why the root cert has an expiry at all
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2145) 'RFC 5280 §4.1.2.5 makes `notAfter` a MANDATORY field on every X.509 certificate. There is no valid X.509 leaf, intermediate, or root that carries no expiry. So the question is not "should the root have an expiry" but "how far out do we set the field that has to be there".
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2145) '    The validity period for a certificate is the period of time from
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2145) '    notBefore through notAfter, inclusive. […] To indicate that a
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2145) '    certificate has no well-defined expiration date, the notAfter
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-2145) '    SHOULD be assigned the GeneralizedTime value of
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-2145) '    99991231235959Z.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-2145) '    (RFC 5280 §4.1.2.5)
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-2145) 'RFC 5280 permits the "never expires" sentinel (`99991231235959Z`) for certs the issuer treats as having no defined end date. Some private CAs use it. This RFD does not, for the reasons below. First, OpenSSL prints `notAfter=Dec 31 23:59:59 9999 GMT` and some X.509 parsers reject the year field as out of range: RFC 5280 requires UTCTime for years 1950-2049 and GeneralizedTime after, so pre-1994 parsers and some embedded stacks fail. Second, the intent ("this cert never expires, we rotate on schedule") is invisible to anyone reading the cert. 25 years is long enough that the field is not the rotation gate, and short enough that every stack we ship to handles it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Why the ROTATION on the root is 5 years, not 25
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Three converging bodies of guidance land in the same window.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-2145) '**Mozilla Root Store Policy §7.1** removes public-trust for a root CA whose key material is more than 15 years old:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-2145) '    For a Root CA certificate trusted for server authentication,
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-2145) '    Mozilla will remove the websites trust bit when the CA key
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-2145) '    material is more than 15 years old.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-2145) '    (Mozilla Root Store Policy v2.9, 2023-09)
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Our root is private, so Mozilla does not force our hand. The industry consensus reflected in that number is that 15 years is the outer edge before cryptographic parameters and operational discipline drift enough to warrant re-mint.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-2145) '**NCSC in-house PKI principles: "keep certificate lifetimes as short as practical".** No fixed year on the root. The phrasing is deliberate. Short as practical, taking rotation cost into account.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-2145) '    Certificates should have lifetimes that are as short as practical,
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-2145) '    while balancing the operational overhead of renewal.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-2145) '    (NCSC "In-house public key infrastructure, PKI principles")
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-2145) '**NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 §5.3.6 (key lifetimes).** Recommends both "originator usage period" and "recipient usage period" bounded. Public-key signing keys used to certify other keys are the highest- value keys in the system and thus have the tightest recommended cryptoperiod. Not a specific year, but a directional bound.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:40Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Five years is the smallest rotation cadence at which the CA ceremony is a set-piece event we prepare for rather than an emergency. Half of that is the "start scheduling the next ceremony" trigger.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:41Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Why the break-glass CA gets the SAME numbers as the root
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:42Z (RFD66606-2145) 'The break-glass CA sits in 1P beside the operator's other credentials and comes out on the day the primary root is unusable. That day is a disaster with a fixed script. The break-glass root immediately signs a new set of intermediates and every downstream leaf. If the break-glass has less validity remaining than the primary rotation cadence, the swap-in re-mints twice (once to swap, once to reach the next scheduled ceremony). That is the class of error a DR runbook cannot afford.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:43Z (RFD66606-2145) 'So the break-glass CA carries the SAME `notAfter` as the primary root (25 years) and the SAME policy rotation window (5 years). It also carries a bigger compromise surface than the primary root because 1P is warmer storage than an offline HSM ceremony. Its audit expectation is stricter: `op` reads are logged, the 1P item is CONCEALED-only (RFD66606-2144 defect #12 was moving the key from `notesPlain` to CONCEALED), and any pull of the private key is followed by a `shred -u` of the scratch file.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:44Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Why the intermediates get 3 years
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:45Z (RFD66606-2145) 'An intermediate CA is online (or at least reachable) whenever it signs a leaf. Its compromise surface is bigger than the root's. Rotating it under 5 years is standard private-PKI practice. 3 years keeps the cadence at "annual re-mint of a new one, run for a year, retire the older one". A two-CA overlap window costs nothing and lets the trust-store update propagate.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:46Z (RFD66606-2145) 'CA/B Forum's SC-081v3 vote (2025-04) sets public TLS leaf lifetimes on a schedule (200 days now, 100 by 2027-03, 47 by 2029-03). The root and intermediate lifetimes for publicly-trusted CAs are already 15 years (Mozilla). Our private intermediates run inside that number, at 1/5 of it, because we control the whole chain and can rotate cheaply.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:47Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Why the leaves get 90 days / 14 days
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:48Z (RFD66606-2145) '**mTLS server + client (services): 90 days.** Aligns with the Let's Encrypt cadence the industry has run for a decade without incident. Matches CA/B Forum's 2027 target for public TLS. Short enough that a compromised leaf is a real cost to the attacker.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:49Z (RFD66606-2145) '**WebTransport leaf: 14 days.** Chrome enforces 14 days as the maximum validity for a certificate cited in `serverCertificateHashes`. Longer and the browser refuses the QUIC handshake. Not a policy choice on our side. It is what the protocol accepts.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:50Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Current state, 2026-09-01
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2145  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-09-01T00:00:51Z (RFD66606-2145) 'Every live cert in the workspace and how it stacks up against this RFD:
